          "text": "Q2 is the sharpest thing raised in this whole thread — the asymmetry you spotted is real and it has a clean resolution.\n\n## 2) Delta form — the frame is wrong, not the mechanism\n\n\"Removed\" doesn't make sense for recency because **you're describing a state diff when the thing you want is an event log.**\n\n- **Pinned** is a *set* whose membership the user controls. Pin and unpin are both real user actions.\n- **Recent** is a *window over a stream*. Nothing is ever removed — things are displaced by newer things. Displacement isn't an event; it's an artifact of a rendering cap.\n\nSo don't emit \"the recent list changed.\" Emit what happened:\n\n| Event | Append? | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| new chat gets a title | ✓ | a thing came into existence |\n| user pins a chat | ✓ | deliberate importance signal |\n| user unpins a chat | ✓ | deliberate downgrade; keeps the picture accurate |\n| chat falls out of the recent-10 | ✗ | **nothing happened** — the cap applies to the baseline rendering only |\n| chat archived / deleted | ✗ | an append would preserve exactly what the user removed |\n\nUnder that frame your objection dissolves: there is no \"removed from recent,\" because the 10-cap is a *baseline rendering budget*, not a live window the model tracks.\n\n**Why not full snapshots:** a snapshot is ~20 entries × (title + 200-char excerpt) ≈ 1400 tokens, written into **permanent history**. Five fires in one chat = ~7k tokens forever. An event append is ~70 tokens. Two orders of magnitude, and the snapshot buys only correctness the baseline already provides.\n\nCumulative growth is bounded by the re-bake — each compaction/model switch resets the log to a fresh capped baseline. Between epochs, growth is capped by the user's chat-creation rate during one conversation, which is small.\n\n## 1) Model-switch re-bake — one marker, not a repeat\n\nThe old baseline lived in the old **system prompt**, which was never in `messages`. So on re-bake it vanishes completely from the model's view — but the **prior deltas are still in history**, and they were computed against a baseline that no longer exists. They become redundant (naming chats now in the new baseline) or misleading.\n\nThree ways to handle it:\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Silent** | New baseline supersedes; stale deltas linger. One prose line in the framing. Cheapest, but the guarantee lives in operator-editable prose. |\n| **B. Supersession marker** ✅ | One-line typed part: *\"the chat list above has been refreshed; disregard earlier chat-list updates in this conversation.\"* ~15 tokens, once per epoch, structural rather than prose-dependent. |\n| **C. Full digest as a delta too** | Duplicates the entire baseline into permanent history. This is the thing we're avoiding. No. |\n\n**Recommend B**, and keep it orthogonal to the model-switch reminder. `renderModelSwitchReminder` says \"you are now running as model X\" — that's about the model. The supersession marker is about the chat list. They fire together on a model switch and render adjacent in the same message; at a **compaction** re-bake only the supersession marker fires, since there's no switch. Merging them would mean inventing a variant for the compaction case.\n\nSo to answer directly: **new system prompt + a one-line supersession marker. No repeated names, no re-listing.**\n\n## 3) Gating — you're right that it's confusing, because it's being modeled as peers\n\nTwo orthogonal questions are being collapsed into one:\n\n1. **May the assistant use my other chats at all?**\n2. **Proactively, or only when it goes looking?**\n\nAs peers that's an intersection the user has to reason about. As a hierarchy it's one rule — and `personalization` already ships this exact shape (`enabled` master, `shareAccountIdentity` sub-toggle withholding one class):\n\n```\nhistorySharing        master, default ON   ──▶ #326 search_conversations\n│                                              #327 recent_chats\n└── historyDigest 