lane.\n\n## The precedent to follow — `personalization`\n\nThis layer is deliberately the **same shape** as the shipped `personalization` capability.\nRead these and mirror their structure, naming, and test layout:\n\n- `apps/api/src/db/schema/personalization.ts` + `personalization.test.ts`\n- `apps/api/src/personalization/` — `personalization-repository.ts`, `.service.ts`,\n  `.controller.ts`, `.module.ts`, `dto/personalization.dto.ts`\n- `apps/api/src/personalization/personalization-rls.integration.test.ts`\n- `apps/api/src/personalization/personalization.integration.test.ts` (HTTP boundary)\n\nMirroring the precedent is the point. Do not invent a different shape.\n\n**But do NOT fold `memory` into the `personalization` table or module.** The spec is\nexplicit that these are separate axes: `personalization.enabled` means \"use my authored\nprofile\", while `shareRecentChats` grants access to conversation history. They must be\nindependently settable, and disabling one must have no effect on the other.\n\n## Declared file set — create/touch these and nothing else\n\nNew:\n- `apps/api/src/db/schema/memory.ts`\n- `apps/api/src/db/schema/memory.test.ts`\n- `apps/api/src/memory/memory-repository.ts`\n- `apps/api/src/memory/memory.service.ts`\n- `apps/api/src/memory/memory.service.test.ts`\n- `apps/api/src/memory/memory.controller.ts`\n- `apps/api/src/memory/memory.module.ts`\n- `apps/api/src/memory/dto/memory.dto.ts`\n- `apps/api/src/memory/dto/memory.dto.test.ts`\n- `apps/api/src/memory/memory-rls.integration.test.ts`\n- `apps/api/src/memory/memory.integration.test.ts`\n- one new migration under `apps/api/src/db/migrations/` plus its `meta/` snapshot,\n  **generated** by `pnpm --filter api db:generate` — never hand-authored\n\nModified:\n- `apps/api/src/db/schema/index.ts` (export the new schema)\n- `apps/api/src/app.module.ts` (register `MemoryModule`)\n- `apps/api/src/db/migrations/meta/_journal.json` (written by db:generate)\n- `apps/api/AGENTS.md` (tasks 2.2 and 2.9 documentation)\n\nIf you believe a file outside this set must change, STOP and say so in your final message\ninstead of editing it.\n\n## Architecture decisions (settled — implement these)\n\n### 1. Schema\n\nOne row per user, owner-keyed, mirroring `personalization.ts`:\n`userId` PK/FK to users with cascade delete, `shareRecentChats` boolean **NOT NULL default\nfalse**, plus the same timestamp columns the precedent carries. Four owner policies\n(select/insert/update/delete) evaluated against `app.current_user_id`, exactly as\n`personalization.ts` writes them. **There is no public-read policy** — unlike `chats`,\nthese settings must never be reachable through the public chat-sharing path or the empty\nidentity. Add the schema unit test mirroring `personalization.test.ts`, asserting the\ncolumn default is `false` and that all four policies exist.\n\n### 2. Migration — the one hand-edit this repo requires\n\nGenerate it with `pnpm --filter api db:generate`. Drizzle's `.enableRLS()` emits only\n`ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`, **never `FORCE`**. You must hand-append\n\n```sql\nALTER TABLE \"memory_settings\" FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;\n```\n\nto the generated migration. This is not optional and not cosmetic: migrations run as the\n`app` role which **owns every table**, and without `FORCE` the owning role bypasses all\nfour policies — which in a single-role self-hosted deployment means no isolation at all.\n`apps/api/AGENTS.md` documents this exact exception for `0004`/`0011`/`0018`/`0021`/`0023`\nand the personalization migration; **add your new migration to that documented list**\n(task 2.2) so a future regeneration re-adds the statement.\n\nDo not hand-write any other migration SQL and do not hand-edit `meta/_journal.json`\nbeyond what `db:generate` writes.\n\n### 3. Repository / service / narrow resolver type\n\nFollow the precedent's layering: repository does DB access inside the tenant scope,\nservice wraps it. **Absence of a row is not a special case** — a user with no row reads as\n`shareRecentChats: false`, identical to one who explicitly disabled it. Implement that in\none place, not at each call site.\n\nExport a **narrow resolver type** for future consumers, following the `PromptUserResolver`\nrecipe in `apps/api/AGENTS.md`:\n\n```ts\nexport type MemorySettingsResolver = Pick<MemoryService, 'getForOwner'>;\n```\n\n`as unknown as T` is **banned repo-wide** and enforced by a staged-file lefthook gate.\nNarrow the dependency type instead — never cast a fixture.\n\n### 4. HTTP surface\n\n`GET` and `PATCH` on `/api/v1/me/memory`, mirroring `PersonalizationController` including\nits `@ApiTags` / `@ApiBearerAuth` / `@ApiCookieAuth` / `@ApiOkResponse` /\n`@ApiUnauthorizedResponse` annotations and its header comment about why the surface is safe.\n\nIdentity comes **only** from `@CurrentUser()`. There must be no route parameter, query\nfield, or body field naming a user — so there is nothing a caller could supply to target\nsomeone else's settings.\n\nThe DTO is class-validator behind the global `ValidationPipe`; the response is an explicit\nresponse type with an egress allowlist (`toMemoryResponse`), never an ad-hoc object.\n\n**Copy the precedent's PATCH semantics exactly, including the reason.** Use a conditional\nspread per field rather than spreading `input`: an unset DTO field is an own `undefined`\nproperty because class fields are defined, so spreading the input would write `undefined`\nover every absent key. Read `PersonalizationController.updatePersonalization`'s comment —\nit explains the trap in full.\n\n## Required tests (tasks 2.5-2.8)\n\nFollow `docs/testing.md`. `.integration.` infix = needs real Postgres.\n\n2.5 unit — default-false when no row exists; partial-update semantics; a client-supplied\nuser id is ignored.\n\n2.6 unit — `shareRecentChats` is never inferred: pinning, searching, or otherwise using\nhistory features leaves it unchanged, and only the PATCH endpoint can alter it. Assert this\nagainst the service surface rather than by asserting on unrelated modules.\n\n2.7 integration, in the RLS suite (mirror `personalization-rls.integration.test.ts`) —\ncross-tenant read denied, cross-tenant update denied and leaves the target's value\nunchanged, and the empty (public) identity reads nothing. These run as the owning role, so\nthey are what actually proves `FORCE` was applied.\n\n2.8 integration at the HTTP boundary (mirror `personalization.integration.test.ts`) —\nunauthenticated request rejected; response body carries no field outside the allowlist.\n\n## Documentation (task 2.9)\n\nIn `apps/api/AGENTS.md`, document that `shareRecentChats` sends titles and opening excerpts\nof the owner's other chats to the operator-configured model provider — which in a\nmulti-user instance may be a third party with no relationship to that user. State **all\nthree** consequences together; the spec is explicit that stating fewer produces an\nincomplete consent contract:\n\n1. **Enabling is retroactive** over the whole existing corpus — chats created long before\n   the setting was turned on become eligible immediately, opening excerpts included.\n2. **Disabling is not retroactive** — it stops new baselines, re-bakes, and appends, but a\n   chat that already carries a baseline keeps sending it.\n3. **Deleting a chat is not erasure** — its title and excerpt survive in other chats'\n   already-bound prompts, in persisted appends, and in receipts already issued.\n\n## Pass condition — all must pass, run them yourself\n\n```bash\npnpm --filter api typecheck\npnpm --filter api test\npnpm --filter api test:integration   # self-provisions Postgres via Testcontainers (docker)\npnpm lint\n```\n\n`test:integration` is the gate that actually proves RLS. If it cannot start Postgres, say\nso explicitly in your final message rather than reporting the layer as done.\n\nThen format ONLY the files you touched:\n`pnpm exec prettier --write <each file you changed>`\nNever run a repo-wide format.\n\n## 