(`baseline`)** stores two per-chat fields: the frozen `recencyDigestBaseline`\n  and the growing `recencyDigestTold` (chat ids plus last-told pin state).\n  `RecencyDigestService.resolveCandidate(ownerUserId, currentChatId)` already returns\n  `{ baseline, told }` where `told` is `{ chatId, pinned }[]` — **that is the shape your\n  set-diff consumes.** `ChatsRepository.setRecencyDigestIfAbsent` is first-writer-wins.\n- Layer 3 also fixed an N+1: excerpts and counts come from\n  `MessagesRepository.findEarliestUserMessagePerChat(chatIds, owner)` and\n  `countPerChat(chatIds, owner)` — **two set-scoped queries for the whole candidate set.**\n  **This layer re-resolves the capped views on EVERY SEND**, so that shape is now\n  load-bearing: do not reintroduce per-chat hydration, and do not add a query inside a loop.\n- Layer 3 put the summarization exclusion in `compaction.ts` already\n  (`STANDING_CONTEXT_EXCLUSION` names both delimiters). Do not touch it.\n\n## What this layer does NOT do\n\nNo compaction re-bake (layer 5 — you add the supersession *part and renderer*, but layer 5\nemits it), and no digest block in `apps/api/src/prompts/chat-default.md` (layer 6).\n\n**This is the first layer where digest content becomes model-visible** — the message rail\ndoes not depend on the packaged prompt. That is exactly why the summarization exclusion\nalready landed underneath you.\n\n## Precedents to follow exactly\n\n- `apps/api/src/chats/model-context-part.ts` — `isModelSwitchPart`, `createModelSwitchPart`,\n  `renderModelSwitchReminder`. Mirror this shape: strict exact-shape validation on\n  authoring, a server-owned renderer, a type guard.\n- `apps/api/src/chats/tool-availability-part.ts` — the second existing example.\n- `apps/api/src/chats/context-builder.ts` around line 312 — the existing reminder slot:\n  ```ts\n  const reminders = [\n    ...(switchPart ? [renderModelSwitchReminder(switchPart)] : []),\n    ...(availabilityPart ? [renderToolAvailabilityReminder(availabilityPart)] : []),\n  ];\n  ```\n  Your append renders here as **one more independent entry**. Task 4.6 is explicit: no\n  combined form, no special-casing. A delta and a model switch on the same turn emit both\n  reminders independently (task 4.7).\n\n## Architecture decisions (settled — implement these)\n\n### 1. Exactly one event type, derived by set-difference (task 4.3)\n\nAn event is: a chat **entered the told-set**, or **its pin state changed** relative to what\nthe told-set records. Never a restated list. Events are derived by comparison against\nstored state, **never from timestamps** — no column records when a chat gained a title, and\nunpinning is a hard row delete that leaves no trace, so a timestamp derivation cannot see\ntwo of the three transitions.\n\n**The two halves read DIFFERENT candidate sets. Conflating them breaks the feature in\nopposite directions:**\n\n- **New entries** come from the **freshly resolved capped views** (top 10 pinned + top 10\n  recent) **minus the told-set**. NOT from the whole eligible corpus — that would append\n  every untold titled chat on the next run, disclosing hundreds of chats for a large owner\n  and defeating the cap the digest exists to enforce.\n- **Pin-state changes** are checked over the **already-told ids only**, against `pins`\n  membership, and are deliberately **NOT** restricted to the capped views — restricting them\n  would miss an unpin of a told chat that has since fallen outside the top 10, leaving the\n  model permanently wrong about it.\n\nCapped views bound what may be **added**; the told-set bounds what may be **corrected**.\n\n**Pin state means membership in the owner's `pins`, never membership in the rendered pinned\nlist.** The two diverge whenever a newly pinned chat pushes another out of the capped\nrendering: the displaced chat is still pinned, and reporting it as unpinned would be false.\nDeriving pin state from the rendered list turns every cap displacement into a fabricated\nunpin.\n\n**The comparison is asymmetric.** A chat that has LEFT the eligible view produces nothing.\nArchival, deletion, and displacement therefore need no rule of their own — each is simply a\ndeparture, and departures are ignored **by construction, not by a special case**.\n\n**The told-set records only chats the model was actually told about.** Resolving the lists\nrequires reading the owner's complete pin set (a capped ordered selection cannot be computed\nfrom a partial one), but that full set is **selection input, not told state**. Recording pin\nstate for a never-told chat would let unpinning it emit an append that introduces the chat\nsolely to demote it — disclosing more than saying nothing.\n\nMultiple events between two runs **batch into a single append**.\n\n### 2. Entry shape and accumulation\n\nAn append carries the **same per-entry shape as a baseline entry**, including the capped\nexcerpt, so an appended chat and a baseline chat are equally usable.\n\n**No ceiling on accumulated appends.** Accumulation is driven by how often the owner starts\nor returns to other chats; re-resolution is driven by the current chat's length — uncorrelated\naxes, so a long-lived low-volume conversation may accumulate appends indefinitely without\nre-baking. This is accepted, not capped. Document the consequence (task 4.10); do not invent\nan adaptive policy.\n\nAppends are persisted with the message they accompany and are never rewritten or retracted.\n\n### 3. Gating (task 4.5)\n\nAuthor the part on the user message in `chat-loop.service.ts`, gated on `shareRecentChats`\n**AND the existence of a baseline** — not the setting alone. A baseline-less chat has no\ntold-set to diff against, so it must not receive an append before its initialization commits.\n\n**Never inspect the prompt template** to decide whether to append. An operator template that\nomits the digest block while the setting is enabled still receives appends; that consequence\nis documented rather than mitigated, consistent with the existing rule that a prompt\nreferencing no per-user path silently forgoes that content.\n\nThe same binding-time discipline as layer 3 applies: the governing setting value is the one\nobserved **inside the binding transaction** under the `FOR SHARE` lock\n(`getForOwnerForBinding`), not the one read while resolving.\n\n### 4. Told-set advance (task 4.4)\n\nAdvance the told-set **in the same transaction as the append it accounts for**, so a run\nthat fails to persist cannot leave the conversation marked as having been told something it\nnever received. A failed transaction leaves the told-set unchanged and the same event is\ndetected again next run.\n\n### 5. Supersession marker (task 4.2)\n\nAdd the part and its renderer here; **layer 5 emits it**. It states that the list has been\nrefreshed and that earlier chat-list updates in the conversation are superseded. No\nsupersession marker on a model switch — nothing is superseded there.\n\n## Required tests (4.7-4.9)\n\nFollow `docs/testing.md`. Unit tests unless a case genuinely needs Postgres.\n\n- 4.7: a delta and a model switch on the same turn emit **both** reminders independently;\n  displacement, archival, and deletion emit nothing; batching collapses multiple events into\n  one append.\n- 4.8: an already-told chat never repeats; a chat resurfacing through ordinary activity alone\n  **does** append; a resurfaced below-cap chat does append; a newly pinned chat displacing a\n  rendered one emits **no** unpin (it is still pinned); unpinning a never-announced chat\n  emits nothing; a failed transaction leaves the told-set unchanged.\n- 4.9: with `shareRecentChats` off, no append is emitted on any turn.\n\n## Documentation (task 4.10)\n\n`apps/api/AGENTS.md` and `docs/scaling.md`: this layer adds a **server-authored message-part\nschema**, which is a coordinated API/worker revision boundary — deploy workers able to render\nit **before** any API authors it; on rollback stop authoring, drain accepted Runs, then roll\nbinaries back. Also document the unbounded-append consequence from §2.\n\n## Pass condition — run them all yourself\n\n```bash\npnpm --filter api typecheck\npnpm --filter api test\npnpm lint\npnpm build                      # regenerates the COMMITTED apps/api/openapi.json\npnpm --filter api test:integration\n```\n\n`pnpm build` is not optional and not redundant: it is the ONLY gate that catches a missing\nNest `@Inject` token on a narrow-typed constructor (the narrow type erases to `Object`, so\ntypecheck and every unit test pass while the app cannot boot) and the only one that\nregenerates the committed OpenAPI document. If this layer changes no HTTP surface, `pnpm\nbuild` should leave `openapi.json` unchanged — verify that rather than assuming it.\n\nTwo integration tests are **known load-sensitive flakes** and are not your regression:\n`chats-messages.integration.test.ts` \"serializes turns per chat\" (`expected 200 to be 409`)\nand `worker-concurrency.integration.test.ts` \"7.1 … wall clock < serial sum\". If only those\nfail, say so and report the rest green. Any other failure is yours.\n\nFormat only the files you touched (`pnpm exec prettier --write <files>`); never repo-wide.\n\n## Conventions that will fail review if ignored\n\n- `as unknown as T` is banned repo-wide (lefthook gate). Narrow the *dependency* type and\n  give it an explicit `@Inject(Token)` — the annotation alone carries no DI metadata.\n- Comments explain **why**, not what. Security-relevant decisions name the threat they close.\n- Keep the diff minimal. No speculative abstraction.\n\n## Final message\n\nReport: files changed, each gate's **actual** exit code, whether `openapi.json` moved, how\nthe two candidate sets are kept distinct in the code, which tests cover the asymmetric\ncomparison (departures emitting nothing) and the pins-vs-rendered-list distinction, anything\nyou could not satisfy, and anything you judged underspecified. Be blunt about what you are\nunsure of.\n",
