          "text": "Base directory for this skill: /home/leon0399/.claude/skills/octocat\n\n# GitHub Skill\n\nUse the `gh` CLI for all GitHub interactions. Never suggest the web UI when `gh` can do it.\n\n## Core mechanics\n\nThese four behaviours cause *silently wrong answers* rather than errors, which is why they lead:\na truncated list and a mis-parsed query both look like a successful, complete result.\n\n### Discover `--json` fields instead of guessing\n\nRun a command with `--json` and **no field list** — it prints every available field, then pick from\nthat list. Saves a guess-and-retry loop and surfaces fields you didn't know existed:\n\n```bash\ngh pr view --json          # prints the full field list, then exits\ngh issue list --json\n```\n\nAdd `--jq '<expr>'` to filter without a separate `jq` process. `--template` (`-T`) shapes text\noutput, but `-T` collides with a body-template flag on `gh pr create` / `gh issue create` — check\n`--help` before using the short form there.\n\n### List commands truncate silently\n\n`gh pr list`, `gh issue list`, and every `gh search` subcommand default to **30 results** and give\nno indication that more exist. Reading 30 of 200 open PRs and reporting \"that's all of them\" is the\nmost common way this skill's output goes wrong.\n\n```bash\ngh pr list -L 200                      # raise the cap explicitly\ngh api repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls --paginate --jq '.[] | .number'\ngh api graphql --paginate --slurp -f query='...'   # --slurp merges pages into one array\n```\n\n`gh pr list` / `gh issue list` do not expose `totalCount` via `--json`. If you need a true total,\nquery it via `gh api graphql`; otherwise treat `-L` as the ceiling for that call and say so.\n\nNested `--json` arrays are capped too, and this one bites hardest because nothing in the output\nsays so. `gh pr view --json files` returns at most 100 entries. On a 115-file PR:\n\n```bash\ngh pr view 140782 -R kubernetes/kubernetes --json changedFiles,files \\\n  --jq '{declared: .changedFiles, returned: (.files | length)}'\n# => {\"declared\":115,\"returned\":100}\n```\n\nThe defence is that the true count usually sits in a sibling scalar field — so ask for both and\ncompare. `changedFiles` pairs with `files`; `subIssues`, `blockedBy`, and `blocking` are shaped\n`{\"nodes\": [...], \"totalCount\": N}` (capped at 100, 50, and 50) and carry their own `totalCount`.\nIf the numbers disagree, page through `gh api --paginate` instead of trusting the array. Saying\n\"this PR touches 100 files\" when it touches 115 is the kind of error nobody catches downstream.\n\n### Target the repo explicitly\n\n`gh` infers the repo from the cwd's git remotes. Pass `--repo OWNER/REPO` (`-R`) whenever you're\nacting on a repo other than the one you're standing in — or whenever you're not certain which that\nis. Do the same for `gh api`: the `{owner}/{repo}` placeholder is auto-filled from detected remotes,\nwhich is convenient interactively and a liability in a script. Write the values literally when the\nanswer needs to be deterministic.\n\n### Don't fight the terminal\n\n`gh` already behaves correctly in non-TTY contexts — it skips the pager, strips color, and fails\nfast with a useful message instead of prompting. There is no `--no-pager` flag and setting\n`GH_PAGER` defensively is unnecessary. If you *want* TTY-style tables and color, set `GH_FORCE_TTY=1`\ndeliberately.\n\n## Reading files from a repo without cloning\n\nTwo calls answer most \"what's in that repo\" questions, and neither needs a working copy. Reach for\nthem before cloning anything — a clone pays for an entire checkout, and the question usually turns\nout to need two or three named files.\n\nStart with structure, so you're choosing paths instead of guessing them. One call returns every path\nwith its blob size:\n\n```bash\ngh api 'repos/OWNER/REPO/git/trees/HEAD?recursive=1' \\\n  --jq '.tree[] | select(.type==\"blob\") | \"\\(.size)\\t\\(.path)\"'\n```\n\nCheck the sibling `truncated` field on enormous repos — it flips to `true` and drops entries with no\nother signal. The ceiling is high enough that even a monorepo usually comes back whole, so this is a\ncheap assertion rather than a routine worry. To scope to one directory instead, the contents endpoint\naccepts a directory path and returns `{type, size, path}` per entry.\n\nThen read the file, asking for the **raw** media type — the default response wraps content in base64:\n\n```bash\ngh api repos/OWNER/REPO/contents/PATH -H \"Accept: application/vnd.github.raw\"\ngh api 'repos/OWNER/REPO/contents/PATH?ref=v1.2.0' -H \"Accept: application/vnd.github.raw\"\n```\n\nPrefer raw over decoding the default, because the base64 form fails *silently* above 1 MB. GitHub\nanswers HTTP 200 with `encoding: \"none\"` and `content` set to the **empty string** — not null, not an\nerror:\n\n```bash\ngh api repos/kubernetes/kubernetes/contents/api/openapi-spec/swagger.json \\\n  --jq '{encoding, decoded_len: (.content|@base64d|length)}'\n# => {\"encoding\":\"none\",\"decoded_len\":0}\n```\n\nSo `--jq '.content | @base64d'` prints nothing and exits 0, which is indistinguishable from an empty\nfile — the same class of silent-wrong-answer as the truncation traps in [Core\nmechanics](#list-commands-truncate-silently). The raw media type returns that same file in full, so\ndefaulting to it means the trap never arises. Add `?ref=` to pin a branch, tag, or SHA;\nwithout it you get the default branch's current tip, which is not reproducible.\n\nWhen the task is *searching* rather than reading — patterns across files you can't name in advance,\ngit history, blame, or diffs between refs — an API call is the wrong shape. Get a local cached\ncheckout via the `librarian` skill and use `rg` / `ast-grep` / `git log` against it.\n\n## Pull Requests\n\n**If the work involves a stack, invoke the `gh-stack` skill and follow it instead of this section.**\nDecide that early, because the decision expires: a stack has to exist *before* the code is written,\nand there is no non-interactive way to split a finished branch afterwards (`gh stack modify` is\nTUI-only). By the time you are reaching for `gh pr create`, the cheap moment has passed.\n\nInvoke it when a stack is **mentioned** — stack, layers, dependent or chained PRs, `gh stack`, or a\nrequest to split work for review — and also when one is **justified** by the shape of the work, even\nunprompted: two or more concerns where a later one depends on an earlier one (schema → API → UI,\nimplementation → its tests), parts with different reviewer audiences, or a part already large enough\nto review on its own. The skill's own test is that a layer you cannot describe in one sentence is\nusually two layers. None of this applies to single-concern work, one-file fixes, or anything that\nreviews fine as one PR, and unrelated changes want a separate stack rather than another layer in this\none. Err toward invoking it: reading the skill costs a few hundred lines, while discovering at review\ntime that five concerns are welded into one branch costs a manual teardown and rebuild. Invoke it\nrather than assuming it is loaded — load order is not guaranteed and it does not fire on `gh pr`\nphrasing.\n\nThree commands below are actively wrong on a stacked branch, not merely suboptimal:\n`gh pr create --base main` sets the wrong base, so the PR's diff swallows every layer beneath it;\n**`gh pr merge` cannot merge a stack at all**, and `--delete-branch` breaks the chain; and\n`gh pr checkout` fetches one branch without its stack, losing local tracking. Use `gh stack submit`,\n`gh stack merge <target> --yes`, and `gh stack checkout` instead. The rest of this section is safe on\na stacked PR — `view`, `checks`, `diff`, and `edit` behave normally, and gh-stack itself points at\n`gh pr edit` for retitling the PRs that `submit --auto` generates. When unsure whether a branch is\nstacked, `gh stack view --json` exits **2** with \"not part of a stack\" and costs nothing.\n\nCreate a PR with a multi-line body (the `--body` flag mangles `\\n` into literal characters — always use `--body-file`):\n\n```bash\ncat > /tmp/pr-body.md << 'EOF'\n## Summary\nDescription here.\nEOF\ngh pr create --base main --head <branch> --title \"<title>\" --body-file /tmp/pr-body.md\n```\n\nIf you must use inline text, use shell ANSI-C quoting: `--body $'line1\\n\\nline2'`\n\nCheck CI status:\n```bash\ngh pr checks <number>\n```\n\nView a PR with specific fields:\n```bash\ngh pr view <number> --json title,state,reviews,statusCheckRollup\n```\n\nCheckout a PR locally:\n```bash\ngh pr checkout 123\n```\n\nView PR diff:\n```bash\ngh pr diff 123\ngh pr diff 123 --name-only  # file list only\n```\n\nMerge a PR:\n```bash\ngh pr merge 123 --squash --delete-branch   # squash merge + cleanup\ngh pr merge 123 --rebase --delete-branch   # rebase merge + cleanup\n```\n\nNeither form works on a stacked PR — `gh stack merge <target> --yes` is the only thing that can,\nand it merges every unmerged PR below the target too, all-or-nothing.\n\nEdit PR metadata:\n```bash\ngh pr edit 123 --title \"New title\" --add-label bug --add-reviewer user1\n```\n\n## CI / GitHub Actions\n\nList recent workflow runs:\n```bash\ngh run list --limit 10\n```\n\nView a failed run and its logs:\n```bash\ngh run view <run-id>\ngh run view <run-id> --log-failed\n```\n\nWatch a run until it completes (use `--exit-status` to get non-zero exit on failure):\n```bash\ngh run watch <run-id> --exit-status\n```\n\n## PR Reviews\n\n### Fetching review threads\n\nDelegate bulk fetching to the **`pr-review-fetcher` agent**. It already owns the paginated GraphQL\nquery and the filtering vocabulary (unresolved, outdated, by author, by date, by file), and it runs\non a cheap model in its own context — so a hundred review threads never land in yours. It is\nread-only by design; come back here for the mutations (reply, resolve, comment) below.\n\n**If you *are* `pr-review-fetcher`**, this paragraph is describing you — run the query yourself and\nignore the delegation. This skill is loaded into that agent, so the instruction would otherwise read\nas \"spawn a copy of me\", which nests pointlessly until it hits the subagent depth limit and burns a\nfull fetch at every layer.\n\nThe reason a dedicated fetcher exists at all: the REST endpoint `/pulls/{number}/comments` does\n**not** expose `isResolved`, and neither does `gh pr view --json` (it has `comments` and `reviews`,\nbut no thread or resolution fields at all). Resolution status is reachable *only* through the\nGraphQL `reviewThreads` connection. Reaching for REST here is the standard failure — it returns a\nplausible-looking list of comments with the one field you needed silently absent.\n\nIf the agent is unavailable, REST still gets you the comment bodies and IDs, but treat every thread\nas unknown-resolution rather than assuming open:\n\n```bash\ngh api repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/123/comments --paginate \\\n  --jq '.[] | {id, path, line, body, user: .user.login, created_at}'\n```\n\n### Fetching review verdicts (approve/request changes)\n\n```bash\n# List all review verdicts\ngh pr view 123 -R owner/repo --json reviews \\\n  --jq '.reviews[] | {author: .author.login, state: .state, submittedAt: .submittedAt}'\n\n# Or via REST\ngh api repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/123/reviews \\\n  --jq '.[] | {id, state, user: .user.login, submitted_at}'\n```\n\n### Submitting a review\n\n```bash\n# Approve\ngh pr review 123 -R owner/repo --approve\n\n# Request changes with comment\ngh pr review 123 -R owner/repo --request-changes --body \"Please fix the null check\"\n\n# Leave a general review comment (not approve/reject)\ngh pr review 123 -R owner/repo --comment --body \"Looks good overall, minor nits\"\n```\n\nFor multi-line review bodies, use `--body-file` (same as PR creation — `--body` mangles newlines):\n```bash\ncat > /tmp/review.md << 'EOF'\nReviewed the auth changes. Two concerns:\n1. Token expiry not handled\n2. Missing rate limit check\nEOF\ngh pr review 123 -R owner/repo --request-changes --body-file /tmp/review.md\n```\n\n### Replying to a review comment\n\nThe reply endpoint requires BOTH the PR number AND the comment ID in the path. This is a common mistake — omitting the PR number gives a 404:\n\n```\nWRONG: repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/comments/COMMENT_ID/replies        (404!)\nRIGHT: repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/PR_NUMBER/comments/COMMENT_ID/replies\n```\n\n```bash\n# Reply to a review comment (PR_NUMBER and COMMENT_ID are both required)\ngh api repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/PR_NUMBER/comments/COMMENT_ID/replies \\\n  -f body=\"Fixed in the latest commit\"\n```\n\nTo find the comment ID to reply to:\n```bash\ngh api repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/PR_NUMBER/comments --paginate \\\n  --jq '.[] | {id, path, line, author: .user.login, body: .body[:80]}'\n```\n\n### Resolving a review thread\n\nResolution requires GraphQL — there is no REST endpoint. You need the thread's GraphQL node ID, the\n`PRRT_...` value, which `pr-review-fetcher` reports on each thread as **Thread**. This is the\n*thread* ID specifically — the comment ID and the comment URL identify a different object, and the\nmutation rejects them.\n\nIf you're resolving something you didn't fetch through the agent, query the IDs directly:\n\n```bash\ngh api graphql -f query='\nquery($owner: String!, $repo: String!, $number: Int!) {\n  repository(owner: $owner, name: $repo) {\n    pullRequest(number: $number) {\n      reviewThreads(first: 100) {\n        nodes { id isResolved path line }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}' -f owner=OWNER -f repo=REPO -F number=123 \\\n  --jq '.data.repository.pullRequest.reviewThreads.nodes[] | select(.isResolved | not) | {id, path, line}'\n```\n\nThen resolve by ID:\n\n```bash\ngh api graphql -f query='\nmutation($threadId: ID!) {\n  resolveReviewThread(input: {threadId: $threadId}) {\n    thread { isResolved }\n  }\n}' -f threadId=\"PRRT_kwDOxxxxxx\"\n```\n\nTo unresolve:\n```bash\ngh api graphql -f query='\nmutation($threadId: ID!) {\n  unresolveReviewThread(input: {threadId: $threadId}) {\n    thread { isResolved }\n  }\n}' -f threadId=\"PRRT_kwDOxxxxxx\"\n```\n\n### Commenting on a PR (top-level, not inline review)\n\nTop-level PR comments use the issues API (PRs are issues in GitHub's model):\n\n```bash\n# Add a comment\ngh pr comment 123 -R owner/repo --body \"Thanks for the review, all addressed\"\n\n# Or with body-file for multi-line\ngh pr comment 123 -R owner/repo --body-file /tmp/comment.md\n```\n\n## Issue types, sub-issues, and dependencies\n\n`gh issue` models these natively — reach for the flags, not the REST endpoints:\n\n```bash\n# On creation\ngh issue create --type Bug --parent 100 --blocked-by 200,201 --blocking 300\n\n# On existing issues (edits one or more issues in the same repo: gh issue edit 23 34)\ngh issue edit 23  --parent 100\ngh issue edit 100 --add-sub-issue 123,124\ngh issue edit 123 --add-blocked-by 200 --add-blocking 300,301\ngh issue edit 23  --type Bug\n```\n\nEach of those has an inverse: `--remove-parent`, `--remove-sub-issue`, `--remove-blocked-by`,\n`--remove-blocking`, `--remove-type`.\n\nRelationship and parent refs are issue **numbers or URLs** — not internal IDs. A URL may point at\nanother repo on the same host; a different host is rejected. `--add-sub-issue` only works when\nediting a single issue.\n\nRead them back through `--json` rather than scraping text output. `gh issue view` and\n`gh issue list` both expose `issueType`, `parent`, `subIssues`, `subIssuesSummary`, `blockedBy`,\nand `blocking`; `gh issue list --type <name>` filters by type. Remember these connections are\n`{nodes, totalCount}` objects with capped `nodes` — see [Core mechanics](#list-commands-truncate-silently).\n\n```bash\ngh issue list --type Bug --json number,title,parent,blockedBy\ngh issue view 42 --json subIssues,subIssuesSummary\n```\n\n<details>\n<summary>REST/GraphQL fallback (GHES only)</summary>\n\nIssue types and sub-issues need GHES 3.17+; blocked-by/blocking need 3.19+. On older servers, or if\na flag is missing, drive the API directly. The trap there is that these endpoints take the\n**internal integer ID**, not the issue number — and `gh issue view --json id` returns the GraphQL\nnode ID, which is a *different* wrong value. Fetch the integer explicitly:\n\n```bash\nCHILD_ID=$(gh api repos/OWNER/REPO/issues/CHILD_NUMBER --jq '.id')\ngh api repos/OWNER/REPO/issues/PARENT_NUMBER/sub_issues -X POST -F \"sub_issue_id=$CHILD_ID\"\ngh api repos/OWNER/REPO/issues/PARENT_NUMBER/sub_issues --jq '.[] | {number, title, state}'\ngh api repos/OWNER/REPO/issues/CHILD_NUMBER/parent --jq '{number, title}'\n\nBLOCKER_ID=$(gh api repos/OWNER/REPO/issues/42 --jq '.id')\ngh api repos/OWNER/REPO/issues/43/dependencies/blocked_by -X POST -F \"issue_id=$BLOCKER_ID\"\ngh api repos/OWNER/REPO/issues/43/dependencies/blocked_by/$BLOCKER_ID -X DELETE\n```\n\nThere is no \"set parent\" endpoint — you add a child to a parent, which is the same relationship\napproached from the other side.\n\n</details>\n\n## Search\n\n`gh search` uses GitHub's search index and takes each qualifier as **its own bare token**. Passing\nthe query as one quoted string is the single most damaging mistake here, because it does not error\n— the whole string is parsed as one keyword and you get an empty result set with exit code 0, which\nreads exactly like \"there are none\":\n\n```bash\ngh search issues repo:cli/cli is:open author:monalisa   # correct\ngh search issues \"label:bug state:open\"                 # WRONG — silently returns nothing\ngh search issues repo:cli/cli \"pagination\"              # quote only multi-word free text\n```\n\nOne exception to \"bare tokens\": quote any qualifier containing a shell metacharacter, or the shell\neats it before `gh` ever sees it. `stars:>1000` unquoted is a **redirect** — it writes a file named\n`1000` and passes an empty `stars:`, which `gh` then rejects with a confusing message about numeric\nvalues. Quote that token alone, not the whole query:\n\n```bash\ngh search repos 'stars:>1000' language:rust   # correct\ngh search repos stars:>1000 language:rust     # WRONG — shell redirect, creates ./1000\n```\n\nMost qualifiers also have a dedicated flag (`--repo`, `--author`, `--label`, …); prefer those when\nthey exist, since a flag value sidesteps both traps. Use `gh search` for anything cross-repo or\nfiltered by author/label:\n\n```bash\ngh search code \"pattern\" --repo owner/repo\ngh search prs repo:owner/repo is:open review:required\n```\n\n`gh issue list --search \"...\"` and `gh pr list --search \"...\"` are the opposite convention: there\nthe query *is* a flag value, so it takes one quoted string — and it is scoped to a single repo.\n\nBots author as GitHub Apps, so `--author dependabot` matches nothing (silently, again). Use\n`--app dependabot` on `pr list` / `issue list` / `search prs|issues`, or `--author \"dependabot[bot]\"`.\n\nAll of these default to 30 results — pass `-L N`.\n\n## Falling back to `gh api`\n\nWhen `--json` genuinely doesn't expose what you need, drop to the API. Check the field list first\n(see [Core mechanics](#discover---json-fields-instead-of-guessing)) — the typed command is usually\nstill the better tool, and dropping to `gh api` prematurely costs you `gh`'s pagination and output\nhandling.\n\nCases where the typed command really can't reach it:\n\n- **Review-thread resolution status** — GraphQL `reviewThreads` only; see [PR Reviews](#pr-reviews).\n- **True totals on lists** — `gh pr list` / `gh issue list` don't expose `totalCount`.\n- **Anything without a subcommand** — `gh api graphql -f query='...' -F var=value`.\n\n```bash\ngh api repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/55 --jq '.title, .state, .user.login'\n```\n\n## Commit Signing\n\n- Never alter `user.signingkey` or signing mode in git config.\n- If signing is already configured, use it as-is.\n- If not configured, proceed without signing — do not set it up.\n\n## Pre-commit Hooks\n\nWhen a hook fails, fix the underlying issue directly rather than bypassing with `--no-verify`. For TypeScript linting issues, delegate to the typescript-magician skill if available.\n\n## Rules\n\n- Validate unfamiliar `gh` subcommands with `gh help <command>` before using them.\n- After opening a PR, watch CI with `gh run watch <run-id> --exit-status` and proactively fix failures.\n\n\nARGUMENTS: file a bug issue on leon0399/llame",
