            "content": "## Summary\n\nServer-authored `data-tool-availability` message parts render as literal debug text in the owner's chat transcript:\n\n```\nunsupported part type: data-tool-availability\n```\n\nThe part is persisted on the user message and returned by the messages API, but `apps/web`'s transcript render switch has no case for it, so it falls through to the catch-all span.\n\nThis is a **shipped defect on `master`**, not a regression from work in flight.\n\n## Impact\n\nOwner-visible. The text appears inside the chat transcript, alongside the owner's own message, and persists across reloads because it is derived from a stored part rather than transient state. It leaks an internal part-type identifier into the product surface.\n\nIt is not a data-integrity or isolation problem — the part carries tool-availability metadata for the model, no cross-tenant content — so severity is cosmetic-but-user-facing.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`apps/web/app/(chat)/components/chat-page.tsx` — the per-part `if/else` chain handles `text`, `reasoning`, tool UI parts, `data-cap-notice`, and `data-model-context`, then falls through to:\n\n```tsx\nreturn (\n  <span key={messagePartKey}>\n    unsupported part type: {part.type}\n  </span>\n);\n```\n\n`data-model-context` got a `return null` case when it was introduced; `data-tool-availability` did not, despite being authored and persisted the same way — see `apps/api/src/chats/chat-loop.service.ts`, where `availabilityPart` is spread into `messageParts` alongside `modelSwitchPart`.\n\nIntroduced by #311 (`feat(api): prepare runtime tool availability`), which added the API-side part without the corresponding web case.\n\nA guard against this exact fallback already exists in `apps/web/app/(chat)/components/chat-page.compaction.test.tsx`:\n\n```ts\nexpect(screen.queryByText(/unsupported part type/i)).toBeNull();\n```\n\nbut it is only asserted inside the model-switch test, so it never covered the new part type. That is why this shipped.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n### A. Deterministic, no infrastructure (recommended)\n\nThe part only renders when it exists on a message, so the fastest repro is a component-level one. On `master`, add to `apps/web/app/(chat)/components/chat-page.compaction.test.tsx` inside the `ChatPage — model context transparency` describe:\n\n```ts\nit(\"repro: availability part prints debug text\", () => {\n  useChatMessages = [\n    {\n      id: \"m1\",\n      role: \"user\",\n      parts: [\n        {\n          type: \"data-tool-availability\",\n          data: { runId: \"11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555\", entries: [] },\n        },\n        { type: \"text\", text: \"Owner question\" },\n      ],\n      metadata: { seq: 1 },\n    },\n  ];\n  renderChatPage(\"chat-repro\", { messages: useChatMessages, compaction: null });\n  expect(screen.queryByText(/unsupported part type/i)).toBeNull(); // FAILS\n});\n```\n\n```bash\npnpm --filter web test\n```\n\nFails with `unsupported part type: data-tool-availability` present in the DOM.\n\n**Note the trap:** the part must go in the `useChat` copy. Putting it only in the authoritative `messages` copy passes against the bug — that copy feeds the separate model-context boundary component, while the *transcript* renders the useChat copy. This is also the copy the persisted parts land in on reload, so it is the faithful repro.\n\n### B. End to end\n\n1. Configure a remote MCP server in `llame.config.json` under `mcpServers` and allowlist one of its tools in `tools.allowed`.\n2. Make that server unreachable (point the URL at a closed port) so the tool resolves unavailable.\n3. Send a chat message. `createToolAvailabilityPart` emits a `kind: 'initial'` part because `unavailable` is non-empty, and it is persisted on the user message.\n4. Reload the chat.\n5. The transcript shows `unsupported part type: data-tool-availability` above the message text.\n\nAny observable availability transition works — a tool added, removed, becoming unavailable, or recovering between runs. A run with no availability change emits no part and does not reproduce.\n\n## Fix\n\nGive the part a `return null` case, matching the `data-model-context` precedent — these are rendered into the model's prompt by the api's `context-builder`, never as chat content.\n\nA fix is already written, but it currently rides the `recency-digest/deltas` layer of the #307 stack, because that layer introduces a second part type (`data-recency-digest`) with the identical latent defect and the same one-line remedy. Flagging it separately here because:\n\n- the `data-tool-availability` half is live on `master` today and need not wait for #307 to merge;\n- a reviewer of a chat-digest stack should not have to discover it also repairs an unrelated shipped bug.\n\nHappy to cherry-pick the web-side commit onto its own PR against `master` if you would rather ship it independently — say the word.\n\nThe accompanying regression test asserts all three server-authored part types render nothing, so the next part type added inherits the guard instead of repeating this.\n",
