        {
          "type": "tool_use",
          "name": "Write",
          "input": {
            "content": "> **Rewritten after design exploration.** The Scope, Acceptance, Boundaries and Cross-links below describe what was **built and shipped**, which reverses four decisions this issue originally argued for: the rail (typed part → system prompt), the content (titles-only → titles plus opening excerpts), identifiers (chat ids included → omitted), and the eval gate (blocking → cut). Each reversal and its reasoning is recorded in place. The Problem statement is unchanged, because it still holds.\n>\n> Normative behaviour lives in [`openspec/changes/add-chat-recency-digest`](../tree/master/openspec/changes/add-chat-recency-digest); this issue is the intent record.\n\n## Problem\n\nRetrieval-only recall systematically under-fires. `search_conversations` requires the model to *decide* to call it, and a model that does not know a relevant chat exists has no reason to look. This is the honest weakness of the #198/#216 design, and it is not fixable inside the tool — the tool is never invoked.\n\nChatGPT's context assembly buys the missing signal by dumping ~40 recent conversations, with message fragments, into every turn unconditionally. That is why it feels omniscient, and it is also the single most expensive, most privacy-surprising, and most injection-exposed thing in the snapshot studied in [`docs/research/long-term-memory/2026-07-27-user-context-injection.md`](../blob/master/docs/research/long-term-memory/2026-07-27-user-context-injection.md) §6. llame should not copy it.\n\nThe middle path: inject **awareness**, retrieve **content**. A bounded list of the owner's other chats gives the model a reason to call `search_conversations`, while the content path stays RLS-scoped, auditable, and framed on arrival.\n\n## Outcome\n\nA capped, owner-scoped digest of the owner's pinned and recent chats is **resolved once per chat and frozen**, rendered into that chat's system prompt, disclosed verbatim in the effective-context receipt, and off by default behind an explicit owner setting. Changes after that first resolution reach the model as appended server-authored events rather than a restated list; compaction is the only boundary that re-resolves it.\n\n## Scope\n\n- **Four fields per entry: title, last-activity date, message count, and a 200-code-point excerpt of the chat's first user message.** Excerpts are in, reversing this issue's original titles-only boundary — titles alone were judged too thin a signal to decide retrieval on. The cap is an **injection and disclosure control, not a token-budget control**: a first user message frequently contains bulk pasted material, and the cap keeps the owner's own leading prose while truncating that payload. Raising it is therefore a security decision.\n- **No chat identifiers.** Reversing the original scope, which included ids. No shipped tool accepts a chat id, so an id would be inert while costing roughly eighteen to twenty tokens per entry — several hundred tokens of unusable text frozen into every prompt. Identifiers belong on transient, requested surfaces that are paid for only when called. Revisit when a tool consumes one (#327/#328).\n- **Rendered into the system prompt, not the reminder rail.** This reverses the original design and the Cross-links rejection below; see that section for why the three objections no longer bind.\n- **Resolved once per chat and immutable thereafter**, re-resolved only at compaction. This is what makes the choice above safe: the rendered prompt is byte-identical across a chat's turns, so the effective-context snapshot is reused rather than re-minted, and the cached prefix survives.\n- Two caps of **10 pinned and 10 recent**, disjoint, pinned rendered first, recent backfilling so it carries a full 10 where the corpus allows. The rendered block states **shown/total ratios** for both lists, so the model can distinguish `10 of 12` from `10 of 247` — nearly complete versus a thin slice — which is exactly the judgment that should decide whether it retrieves.\n- **Eligibility**: excludes the current chat, archived chats, and untitled chats. A chat's title is the eligibility gate; because titling is asynchronous, a chat becomes eligible when its title exists and not before, which yields exactly one first appearance per chat. Pins are filtered to `item_type = 'chat'`.\n- **Changes are appended as events, never as a restated list**, on the existing reminder slot beside the model-switch and tool-availability reminders. Exactly one event type: a chat entered the told-set, or its pin state changed. Departures — archival, deletion, cap displacement — emit nothing by construction.\n- Owner-scoped at the datastore, unreachable through the public/shared-chat path, failing closed when identity is absent.\n- **Off by default**, behind `shareRecentChats` on a new owner-scoped `memory` settings surface kept deliberately separate from `personalization` (see #326 comment).\n- Disclosed verbatim in the owner-visible effective-context receipt.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- [x] ~~An eval decides this.~~ **Cut deliberately.** The eval gate was dropped on the judgment that industry signal for this pattern is already sufficient, and that a blocking eval would have delayed a bounded, default-off, owner-gated feature behind a measurement apparatus that does not exist yet. Recorded as a decision, not an oversight — the feature ships unproven on fire-rate, and that is the accepted trade.\n- [x] Rendered output carries only the four fields and no chat identifier, asserted by test.\n- [x] A title or excerpt containing instruction-shaped or tag-shaped text cannot escape its framing: `<user_chat_history>` is reserved in the authored-text sanitizer and its web mirror, and entries pass through the same tag-balance rules as owner-authored text.\n- [x] The digest is absent on the unauthenticated/shared-chat path, and one owner's digest never contains another owner's chats — proven in the RLS suite with another identity set and with the empty identity.\n- [x] The owner's receipt discloses the rendered digest verbatim and exposes no host path or provider internal.\n- [x] With no baseline bound, the rendered prompt is **byte-identical** to the same template with the digest section removed — no residual framing prose, matching personalization's omission rule.\n- [x] Prefix-cache behaviour is preserved by freezing rather than by position: the prompt does not change between turns, so nothing is invalidated.\n- [x] Enabling is retroactive over the whole existing corpus; disabling is not retroactive; deleting a chat is not erasure. All three are disclosed together in the API contract, because stating fewer is an incomplete consent contract.\n\n## Boundaries\n\nNo embeddings, no semantic ranking, no inferred memory, no new memory store, and no change to `search_conversations` itself. Ranking is recency only — recency is a ranking signal, never a retention or deletion signal.\n\nTwo limits are explicitly **not** structurally enforced, and are recorded rather than glossed:\n\n- The data-not-instructions framing is advisory, carried by the packaged prompt's prose and by model compliance. Only the delimiter's integrity is guaranteed. An operator replacing the packaged prompt may reshape or remove the framing.\n- The compaction exclusion is an instruction telling the summarizing model not to carry the block into its checkpoint. The structural fix — withholding the digest from the summarization input — is foreclosed by putting the digest in the system prompt.\n\nShipped without rendered-markdown egress hardening (`allowedImagePrefixes`/`urlTransform`, `img-src`/`connect-src` CSP). The digest does not create that channel but raises what a successful injection could move through it.\n\n## Cross-links\n\n- #216 is the deliberate-recall proof; this issue is the **awareness** half of the same loop. It does not replace or close #216.\n- #198 owns temporal semantics, provenance, and recency ranking inside the tool. This issue adds nothing to the tool.\n- #326 — the settings hierarchy this resolves; see the comment there.\n- #327/#328 — surfacing chat identifiers and a chat-id filter on the search tool, which is what would make an identifier in the digest worth its tokens.\n\n### Why the system-prompt rail, having been rejected here\n\nThe original text rejected it on three independent grounds. Two were dissolved by the frozen-baseline design; one remains and is mitigated rather than eliminated.\n\n- **Snapshot churn** — dissolved. The objection assumed a per-turn digest minting a full-prompt row on every turn. The digest is resolved once per chat and frozen, so a chat mints one snapshot and reuses it.\n- **Prefix-cache forfeiture** — dissolved, for the same reason. Position-0 volatility is the problem; a byte-identical prompt is not volatile. Re-baking happens only at compaction, which rewrites the conversation anyway.\n- **Trust escalation** — real, and it stands. Conversation-derived text in the system role is an injection privilege-escalation primitive. It is mitigated by the reserved delimiter, the tag-balance sanitizer, explicit framing prose, and the 200-code-point excerpt cap — and the mitigation is documented as advisory rather than structural, which is the honest description.\n\nThe reversal is worth the trade because freezing is what buys it: the reminder rail was chosen originally to protect the cached prefix from a digest that changed every turn, and a digest that does not change every turn does not need that protection.\n",
