          "name": "Write",
          "input": {
            "content": "#307 has shipped the settings decision this issue asked to be made explicitly, so recording the resolution here before it becomes archaeology.\n\n**The question this issue raised:** *\"whether this hangs off the `personalization.enabled` master switch at all … The likely correct resolution is that history-sharing is a **different axis** from authored-profile and should not be gated by `enabled` — but that is a schema and semantics decision that is expensive to reverse, so make it explicitly.\"*\n\n**Resolved: a different axis.** #307 adds an owner-scoped `memory` capability — its own table, its own `/api/v1/me/memory` endpoints, its own RLS policies — carrying `shareRecentChats`. It is **not** gated by `personalization.enabled`, and the two are independently settable in both directions. The reasoning, normatively in `openspec/changes/add-chat-recency-digest/specs/memory/spec.md`: `personalization.enabled` means *\"use my authored profile\"*, and withdrawing a history capability because an owner cleared their profile is a regression they did not request and cannot see. That is the same argument this issue makes for `search_conversations`.\n\n**What that leaves for this issue.** The surface exists, so #326 extends it rather than creating one:\n\n- A master history-access setting composes **above** `shareRecentChats` as a plain conjunction. `shareRecentChats` was deliberately worded as *\"share my recent chats with the assistant\"* rather than *\"the assistant may use my chat history\"* precisely so the broader statement stays available to the setting this issue specifies. That was a naming choice made for this issue's benefit, not an accident.\n- `MemoryService` already exports two narrow resolvers: `MemorySettingsResolver` for ordinary reads, and `MemorySettingsBindingResolver` for the bind-time read. The second is worth noting here, because this issue specifies bind-time reading as the only correct read point.\n\n**One trap #307 hit that this issue will hit too.** Reading the setting inside the binding transaction is *not* sufficient on its own. The repo runs Postgres' default `READ COMMITTED`, so an unlocked re-read still leaves a window: the owner can disable between the read and the commit, and the Run binds under a value that was already false. `getForOwnerForBinding` takes a `FOR SHARE` lock for exactly that reason, which serialises the two orders — a disable that wins first is seen, and a bind that wins first blocks the disable until it commits. Since this issue's setting decides what goes into the immutable bound snapshot, the same lock applies, and `getForOwnerForBinding` is reusable as-is.\n\n**The defaults differ deliberately, and the asymmetry is coherent.** `shareRecentChats` defaults **false**; this issue's setting defaults **true**. Not an inconsistency: #307 moves conversation-derived content the owner never authored for that purpose, unprompted and with no action signalling intent, so consent must be affirmative. This issue gates a capability that already ships and that the owner triggers by asking a question — defaulting it off would be a silent capability removal. Different defaults follow from *who initiates* and *whether anything new is disclosed*, and it is worth stating so the next setting added to `memory` picks its default on that basis rather than by matching whichever neighbour it happens to sit beside.\n\nNothing here pre-empts #133/#45 — `memory` remains a plain owner-scoped boolean surface that becomes an ordinary input to the policy engine later.\n",
